#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114922 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "0d6c1aa8-a2d1-5104-8e27-686c7dd16808" :question/math-academy-id "e-3527" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sec x+4=0$ for $0 \\leq x <             2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\sec x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x =  \\dfrac{1}{(-2)}=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$ Since $\\cos{x} < 0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/0e370925faa2a4abc7ac613657ecdf27c2fa0eaa66cff05b077f77a71999c742.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{8\\pi^2}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114923 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "62e4b12a-19ed-5316-a395-b67da1313687" :question/math-academy-id "e-3529" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x={\\sqrt{2}}$ for $-180^\\circ \\leq  x <            180^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 45^\\circ.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$ Since $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution $x_2$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $360^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_2 = 315^\\circ - 360^\\circ = -45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=45^\\circ,-45^\\circ.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 = 45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114924 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "cc5f8fe7-fd1c-52f9-9dfd-db9ed0a2d973" :question/math-academy-id "e-3532" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi?$\n\n- $\\sec x  = -1$\n\n- $\\sec x  = 2$\n\n- $\\sec x  = \\dfrac 1 3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\sec x$ for $0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\sec x \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1,\\infty).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\sec x = k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114967 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "a3b5986b-17df-5d36-aa1f-6d7d0ef35c85" :question/math-academy-id "e-4260" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x= 3$ for $0 \\leq x <                2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2,$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})=1.230\\,959…≈1.231\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.231\\,0$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 1.231\\,0.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0.$ Since $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/50df7e600a9350f2413da4060293116cccf770df200e55f6534d953d68df42ba.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.231\\,0 \\\\  & =5.052\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 \\approx 1.231\\,0 \\cdot 5.052\\,2 \\approx 6.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a615638-b235-5dbb-9f57-dcff1d743fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277805} {:answer/id #uuid "09109bdc-22cb-5df6-9abe-b92b3bf86d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277806} {:answer/id #uuid "418c406c-b628-54c7-bd70-2c29457b412b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277807} {:answer/id #uuid "05d3cc85-6bb9-54a3-aaaa-448a64b11ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277808} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b6a924-ad2f-5164-b6e4-a8598dce1d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05d3cc85-6bb9-54a3-aaaa-448a64b11ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277808} :answer-field/id #uuid "63758ecc-6ec3-5da6-85d9-c4a815a4e3e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9fb32491-b445-5fcb-aa6f-323a9d2a0ff4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120456" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\sqrt{5}=-\\sqrt{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\sqrt{5} & =-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(-\\sqrt{5})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5})=2.034\\,443…≈2.034\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.034\\,4$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.034\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.107\\,2.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.107\\,2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/f659a13a5a4c9ae998c33b398b777decc8a5603fd18b5579ddebc897ea3dafca.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.107\\,2 \\\\  & =4.248\\,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2.034\\,4\\cdot 4.248\\,7≈8.6,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a22f938f-0fc8-599a-9949-1bb024d374cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277812} {:answer/id #uuid "b1815d22-86d3-532b-ad4f-ce00ea66e49c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277813} {:answer/id #uuid "779ec274-c728-5729-af3f-5312c455aaa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277814} {:answer/id #uuid "3c30cd10-d947-5b5f-a170-626be7bc2a01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277815} {:answer/id #uuid "35be4a37-e374-5ecd-9063-2abeff8aab05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a22f938f-0fc8-599a-9949-1bb024d374cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277812} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c954d3-ee47-5f53-a702-30dd2088dd72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "129a7963-e7f1-51c5-8a9e-c69dd218f1f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120476" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{6}{7}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, equations I, II, and III have no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d312aac3-584a-5fbd-9a37-44e8f9b8e2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277819} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8c9efd-0d7b-5eba-83ed-3c1ea89774b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277820} {:answer/id #uuid "1c654d9e-afff-5be7-a40e-455dc8afcf4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277821} {:answer/id #uuid "fb317fac-de29-5bfb-9deb-ad0253191a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277822} {:answer/id #uuid "19cf6bde-55ed-5adb-a639-ba9670f8f3b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb317fac-de29-5bfb-9deb-ad0253191a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277822} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de5fb98-a523-51c8-aaab-cc7a06ba8d33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dc4e71e-b2cc-535e-8704-1528710848d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120481" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}$$$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{3}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{5}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dda94493-c471-546d-8970-9a3c88cfbd8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277826} {:answer/id #uuid "8968d36e-f925-5484-9993-94217c94ac8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277827} {:answer/id #uuid "4618869e-8840-50c6-8414-e250cbd4245f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277828} {:answer/id #uuid "0f89fb15-cfbd-5ec2-beac-090f32988435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277829} {:answer/id #uuid "c8565c3e-0cbb-5957-bae0-92daa84af11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4618869e-8840-50c6-8414-e250cbd4245f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277828} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fc854fe-cbae-52f4-8ac4-73e10ff0abd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7525e19-691a-53aa-a325-4de37239266a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120487" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\n- $4\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-7=3+2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+3=1$ $$\n- $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-5=-6$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=5$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8015c35-1438-5f46-bce0-c045ff46ab28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277833} {:answer/id #uuid "6e320f97-d10d-590e-9abf-832310f813da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8015c35-1438-5f46-bce0-c045ff46ab28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277833} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb897ae-ad55-5d50-9379-b17eb38cebf9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277832} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cbfd87e-987c-5d00-8471-29324e914989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cbfd87e-987c-5d00-8471-29324e914989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277836} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd9de2b-4327-5617-957b-92d2fc5d7a73" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efe0ceba-4ecd-5baa-8135-9d9e9b625fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-274841" :question/problem "Find the **exact** solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order**, the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/17803dc8d1d3ca2e31586969797b401d5d0a2d958bd6628387b512974076df7a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f180271-5813-5675-af96-87a62fd5b4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f180271-5813-5675-af96-87a62fd5b4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277839} :answer-field/id #uuid "698f3a74-cd06-56b3-9a8a-5066d31165a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277838} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e10babe8-be76-5d41-a5ba-9f5c9fbb8bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e10babe8-be76-5d41-a5ba-9f5c9fbb8bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277841} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9bc9efc-d6b2-5b95-a4ae-5c4495b26294" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15a54511-44fe-53d8-abbd-3c1eb65e9175" :question/math-academy-id "q-274900" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}})={30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={30}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/3f3f1422eca9190a5e0827da456315ba88b074f0bf5e9aab1bb325d131bf1984.png)\n\nAnother solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={330}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-30\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=30\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9a2508d-1cfe-5057-954d-a4d15f0271ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277844} {:answer/id #uuid "b2afbe3f-808e-5f9d-8827-01707cd9ce1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277845} {:answer/id #uuid "ce62c64e-ac42-58c7-af6d-6b19c931f3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277846} {:answer/id #uuid "fae0e687-191c-5bd0-bcdc-596055ff5915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277847} {:answer/id #uuid "43a6f777-6799-57d4-a5e2-27b8859480b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9a2508d-1cfe-5057-954d-a4d15f0271ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277844} :answer-field/id #uuid "02ad47a4-ad1c-5dd4-b55d-a462f6796776" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e6aa2e6-3374-55d1-b3c7-5bb059323dce" :question/math-academy-id "q-28902" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1=3$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1 & =3 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/318eba038feb091d6e3adb80ddf325de6039bb331a7713cf1e2c02171a6388d4.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "864aea57-b86b-5fa1-b79b-2bcab7884d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277851} {:answer/id #uuid "50aa02dd-8989-56a8-bba1-436590b4b0ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277852} {:answer/id #uuid "e181af48-a576-534e-9864-8db8815d8a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277853} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae971ba-a269-5222-9a85-88dad8df0e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277854} {:answer/id #uuid "d498d86c-e081-522b-89c3-fe045c2d2f5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50aa02dd-8989-56a8-bba1-436590b4b0ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277852} :answer-field/id #uuid "1093e2e6-7722-5568-8757-583bc7d345a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "05c4151f-8bb2-5b70-8fe7-236802b47fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-28993" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={45}^{∘},{315}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=45\\cdot 315=14\\,175.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6d89388-dd26-59c2-b62c-aef3350e1be1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113145} {:answer/id #uuid "11d216c9-6d94-57de-b115-a304334c27d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113146} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfd62a7-b472-54ac-a2ee-4b72cf5d625f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113147} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0a8a4e-9945-5e0d-aa15-36e66b5ab94e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113148} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf0683d-0d8a-5267-b81d-502d2a83be7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6d89388-dd26-59c2-b62c-aef3350e1be1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113145} :answer-field/id #uuid "4977aa86-5719-5551-a348-c70c6f677e2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fcb31a9b-9679-5d24-8bf7-7929bd2e8a6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-29163" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=1$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85f71382-728b-5b8b-9de9-41d0876520b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113151} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f32c4e-7771-5957-8ac0-0ca678bf84e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113152} {:answer/id #uuid "52c08603-b802-5655-b554-799dd5122d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113153} {:answer/id #uuid "59198642-9920-523a-81da-7b27e3958713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113154} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce2a871-e4f9-56be-b8d4-a65ddc30fff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9f32c4e-7771-5957-8ac0-0ca678bf84e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113152} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fe12f89-ccd6-5b95-9c23-fb0cd5193b63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08d793ca-18a4-5b84-a226-6c11cd4165db" :question/math-academy-id "q-29177" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={60}^{∘},-{60}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=60\\cdot (-60)=-3\\,600.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88a2376c-dc19-5dd0-a77a-56366a05b81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113157} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a28036-b367-589e-9512-aab611b56f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113158} {:answer/id #uuid "d3998ecb-9d96-51bb-a80c-b98dba07a111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113159} {:answer/id #uuid "1afc57e2-95e9-5005-8244-a42d7d643df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113160} {:answer/id #uuid "64c56874-5776-54fe-a57b-179c6f752f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3998ecb-9d96-51bb-a80c-b98dba07a111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113159} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e1aae67-1413-5c52-92c2-e130fc7ee0d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20eb540b-84ee-5a09-81e9-e0f110dad1d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-29179" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={60}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={60}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X={300}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-{60}^{∘},-{300}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-60\\cdot (-300)=18\\,000\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "850c98a8-4c44-569f-9779-19801333aaf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277858} {:answer/id #uuid "6c75fafa-3b73-5504-bb1b-47983aa24dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277859} {:answer/id #uuid "5d744648-c075-59af-a076-09a1fa55b101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277860} {:answer/id #uuid "95f4c7fa-d2c2-52cf-95f6-4969d599204d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277861} {:answer/id #uuid "56d8b759-364f-5c68-917b-92dd301ccbe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d744648-c075-59af-a076-09a1fa55b101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277860} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4d6c5a6-7bbd-5313-b64a-22e75ed00152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23dbd8da-da31-5df7-bb91-fad8d1a7bceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-29182" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={30}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={30}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/8c6e3ae6bdbfb1c66aee719f77225453992fffe6f3c6e3dfdc3f1ff92c59863a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X={330}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required dommain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={330}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-{30}^{∘},-{330}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-30\\cdot (-330)=9\\,900\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af905be5-8c25-5e8f-8457-c1bc4a2b8c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277865} {:answer/id #uuid "4d038bad-45af-5d5a-a8ac-caf8ac71f485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277866} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3c28fc-5644-5a60-99ce-980fcae5f06d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277867} 