$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x={120}^{∘},{240}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot 240=28\\,800.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "367fe7ab-1252-5bf3-8468-e7b19844d621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,653" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277872} {:answer/id #uuid "78736c4f-1f53-5d5c-93de-0aef30ded33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277873} {:answer/id #uuid "a1171f0f-04f7-593e-9435-ca31eedf1ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23\\,642" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277874} {:answer/id #uuid "062d4e2d-c8ad-5cdd-8c0c-9eec97d6b1c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,876" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277875} {:answer/id #uuid "1f22aaed-940f-5764-9657-0035c0f5e9ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,894" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1171f0f-04f7-593e-9435-ca31eedf1ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23\\,642" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277874} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ee720a-4bc4-56fe-975d-e6705c7bb88e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "285d836c-d6b2-57e5-9c9a-74f208e12b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-30468" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-4)=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-4) & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-16 & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{16})=86.416\\,678{…}^{∘}≈{86.417}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${86.417}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={86.417}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={86.417}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={86.417}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/15e99d348206191535a2b6fb9d85893e666abd16f5f9fc61fdf4e19424e2fde9.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{86.417}^{∘} \\\\  & ={273.583}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=86.417\\cdot 273.583≈23\\,642,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6022a7a-f852-55bf-bb4b-9acf8b8512df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277879} {:answer/id #uuid "36f4ba3d-e1d9-5916-84c5-d4d4a76b5209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6022a7a-f852-55bf-bb4b-9acf8b8512df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277879} :answer-field/id #uuid "165f6e76-65f1-5666-bafa-ac1e4c15bc48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01482d2c-d5c2-54d7-932b-1cd30760092f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01482d2c-d5c2-54d7-932b-1cd30760092f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277882} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d1a995-a765-56e3-837a-7594d151b089" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d736f3dc-378b-533b-985b-098a4888328b" :question/math-academy-id "q-324392" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{4}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(\\frac{3}{4})≈0.723\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈0.723.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈0.723.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/3b95f4a3d92d2214472f8c72f9322f6d1aa89a6e2c4f8cc2ae9f13015217a84d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -0.723 \\\\  & ≈5.560\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=0.7$ and $x=5.6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "616c1241-11ea-5aee-8dd0-0fdba6656d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277885} {:answer/id #uuid "4671674d-d300-5dac-8d2a-6c7134757439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4671674d-d300-5dac-8d2a-6c7134757439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277886} :answer-field/id #uuid "cac192cd-5660-5cfc-995b-620c521d75d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a8a757b-3fc2-5e6a-9e31-629d52bf1e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "244.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a8a757b-3fc2-5e6a-9e31-629d52bf1e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "244.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277888} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec00fe1-72ae-5414-ba1f-56781b77c547" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb05d369-7730-510a-afde-7896782f6715" :question/math-academy-id "q-324397" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2.3$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2.3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{1}{2.3})≈{115.771}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈{180}^{∘}-{115.771}^{∘}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/974fad6534267df035b759df9064ed7dfaff80cb299c41744e8888f248ed1eee.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈{180}^{∘}+{64.229}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{244.229}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=115.8\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=244.2\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0379519a-f193-50a9-868b-84dd5b4253d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0379519a-f193-50a9-868b-84dd5b4253d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277891} :answer-field/id #uuid "591fbe4f-aafd-5d81-bbe7-8f0c605581f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277890} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8983d381-2a60-5879-b957-a3ac8f7ef730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8983d381-2a60-5879-b957-a3ac8f7ef730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277893} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6a37fc8-abfa-5d16-a531-194386fd17fb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aca43237-2858-5ef6-b0b1-f24a1198a232" :question/math-academy-id "q-324401" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})≈2.062\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈\\pi -2.062≈1.080.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈1.080.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/c8020c19d17f3e79b825cb4e0cad4471e68ec0e2618a12578115a0fc8c014599.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi +1.080 \\\\  & ≈4.222\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=2.1$ and $x=4.2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57bb8a21-2552-5dd0-bdf4-ff91c64b6ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57bb8a21-2552-5dd0-bdf4-ff91c64b6ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277896} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4e5aa6f-9a17-54a6-ae93-a67e558f8d79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3de62f0d-cac3-5cfc-9ebf-f32d6295f8e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3de62f0d-cac3-5cfc-9ebf-f32d6295f8e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277898} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a383ec2-309e-5dad-9ff2-55d13cc4f6d9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71b4b55b-5a45-5f2c-88ad-262e17c40ef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-325125" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $(1+\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\sqrt{2}\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/cf16066cb8b8368129be39a22f5c15727ac698f44d6f870511c2e07ec317d643.png)\n\nAnother solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-45\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=45\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5b104c4-d1ef-5d68-ad94-4e6b8106760e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277901} {:answer/id #uuid "522e70ee-798b-5b0d-8704-08d43193ebb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277902} {:answer/id #uuid "9db49764-06d5-534d-b50f-55592d0d83ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277903} {:answer/id #uuid "566a7d4b-e77e-55ad-83a8-44129c098e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277904} {:answer/id #uuid "f541561c-b685-514a-8586-3c8f378c37f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f541561c-b685-514a-8586-3c8f378c37f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277905} :answer-field/id #uuid "e61d4e65-ad94-5bbf-8c32-f580e2a746bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e382d847-b095-5055-a3a9-70bf6b257ece" :question/math-academy-id "q-76488" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={150}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${150}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={150}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/d61c6dbd94be14f2a455791a584a00f19ab7b52452b3acda984c5b278a5e39ed.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={150}^{∘},{210}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=150\\cdot 210=31\\,500.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64844171-b49b-5f4d-b84b-531246e88255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277908} {:answer/id #uuid "619b1d7b-0ab7-5ac2-8286-5495138c75e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277909} {:answer/id #uuid "78034b41-35c9-5063-8bb1-860f3e356084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277910} {:answer/id #uuid "2514b3f7-9bbe-5577-a254-e5f4adf577e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277911} {:answer/id #uuid "4663f6ec-24a7-5700-92f8-55f854484cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "619b1d7b-0ab7-5ac2-8286-5495138c75e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277909} :answer-field/id #uuid "efea3ac5-0d81-5a63-a20d-e71470f42728" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a83238f9-a8b1-5193-9f12-9adee2517d46" :question/math-academy-id "q-76503" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0$ $$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=8$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{7}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation I has no solutions."}]}
